presumptive democratic nominee joe biden  is expected to announce in the coming  days who his vice presidential nominee  will be  this says president trump is floating  the idea of postponing the election  with more on both stories now we are  joined by cbs news political  correspondent ed o'keefe in  washington ed let's start with the  potential vp pick  for vice president biden what do we know  about the vetting process right now i  know we've heard there's still a little  more than 10 women  that are involved that's right about  10 11 women depending on how you count  it uh governors  lawmakers former obama administration  officials  most of whom we believe have been asked  to submit sensitive documents about  their personal finances  the history of their family their public  speeches legislation they've  proposed or voted for in the past all of  this leading up to an announcement that  we're told is unlikely  next week but very likely the week after  which would be just the week before  the democratic convention is supposed to  get underway  we also believe that biden will begin  interviewing some of the finalists in a  bid to sort of shrink that list  in the coming days whether he's going to  do that in person  or virtually he wouldn't say and boy oh  boy  we're trying to figure that out and who  who is helping him make this pick  so he's got at least four people who are  officially doing this  his former general counsel from when he  was vice president  his good friend senator chris dodd  former senator chris dodd of connecticut  the los angeles mayor eric garcetti and  congresswoman lisa blunt rochester  from delaware that states only  congresswoman the first african-american  woman elected to congress from the first  state  they have been doing the paperwork  gathering the initial interviews  they're leading teams of volunteer  attorneys who are doing deeper vets  but beyond them he's going to consult  his wife dr jill biden  his sister valerie and about two or  three aides who've been with him since  the 1970s this will be a very close hold  decision you try to talk to people  across the campaign who admit  they don't know much about what's going  on right now what's in his head  that's partly because of the pandemic  and they're literally not around him  but also because they know he keeps  close counsel and  any indication though i feel silly  asking this now after you said that but  i'm going to ask anyway any indication  about what has become the most important  trait or traits to him  in this candidate this is important  because there's been a lot of  conversation about oh this person would  help them when this state or this group  of people likes this person or  she would bring this kind of experience  remember what biden has said  publicly since the start of this he is  looking for someone with whom he can  have  an equally close personal and  professional relationship as he did with  barack obama  and somebody he believes can step into  the job if god forbid something happens  those are the two most critical things  we've spent some time in the last few  days talking to people who've been part  of  the search process for previous  candidates and they emphasize those are  usually the only two things that matter  can this person step into the job and  how close the relationship would they  have with the president  all other things are far less critical  and history shows  picking this person isn't necessarily a  big factor  in who voters ultimately vote for  perhaps the exception of that being  in recent years when john mccain picked  sarah palin  because there was so much concern that  palin perhaps wasn't ready for the job  all right and finally ed not that  anyone's counting but 93 days  until the election um from the polling  you've seen in the reporting you've done  how many people are still making up  their minds  oh very few uh you know only about like  five six percent of voters roughly at  this point it's not who you're going to  vote for but  how you're going to vote uh will you  vote absentee can you vote absentee  or are you willing to stand in line and  and we should repeat again  because we've said it before we will say  it again the president thinks he can  move the election  he cannot do that without congress  acting and lawmakers say they have no  interest in moving the date  that is correct that is a congressional  call ed o'keefe thank you very much
 

